{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 14, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "the social network is a masterclass in modern filmmaking, blending razor-sharp dialogue, a hypnotic score, and razor-thin character dynamics into a near-perfect cinematic experience. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the film dissects the birth of facebook with the precision of a scalpel, exposing both the genius and the moral rot that accompanies ambition.\n\nthe story follows mark zuckerberg (played with icy detachment by jesse eisenberg) as he transforms harvard’s elite social circles into a digital empire, all while alienating friends, lovers, and business partners along the way. the narrative unfolds through a series of flashbacks, interweaving zuckerberg’s rise with legal battles against the winklevoss twins (armie hammer in a dual role) and eduardo severin (andrew garfield), whose claims of betrayal and stolen ideas frame zuckerberg as both villain and visionary. it’s a tale of betrayal, betrayal, and more betrayal, all lubricated by the cold calculus of silicon valley’s early days.\n\neisenberg’s performance is electric—his mark zuckerberg is a bundle of nervous energy, equal parts intellect and insecurity, a man who speaks in rapid-fire barbs while struggling to connect emotionally. hammer shines as the twin brothers, delivering a performance that oscillates between aristocratic smugness and simmering rage, while garfield’s eduardo is the tragic heart of the film, a loyal friend undone by zuckerberg’s ruthlessness and the machinations of sean parker (justin timberlake, in a scene-stealing turn). timberlake’s parker is a serpent in a hoodie, oozing charisma and menace as the napster co-founder who whispers poison into zuckerberg’s ear, accelerating both facebook’s growth and its moral decay.\n\nthe film’s dialogue is legendary, sorkin’s words crackling with wit and venom. scenes unfold like verbal chess matches—zuzerberg and eduardo’s late-night coding sessions, the deposition room where witnesses twist the truth, the climactic courtroom showdown—all driven by dialogue that’s as quotable as it is cutting. the pacing is relentless, the tension almost unbearable as alliances fracture and egos collide.\n\ntrent reznor and atticus ross’s score is a haunting, synth-driven soundscape that mirrors the film’s themes: isolation, obsession, and the sterile beauty of technology divorced from humanity. the cinematography, by jeff cronenweth, bathes the film in a cool, digital-blue palette, evoking the sterile glow of computer screens and the hollow triumph of virtual connections.\n\nif the film has a flaw, it’s that it sometimes feels too enamored with its own cleverness, letting zuckerberg’s bitterness and the legal drama overshadow the emotional core. the romance with his girlfriend, for instance, feels underdeveloped, a casualty of the film’s laser focus on ambition. but these are minor quibbles in a film that otherwise operates with near-perfect precision.\n\nin the end, the social network is more than just a biopic or a business drama—it’s a dissection of modern masculinity, ambition, and the cost of connection in an increasingly digital world. it’s a film that demands rewatching, not just to catch the dialogue’s nuances but to marvel at how fincher and sorkin turned the birth of a social media empire into a tragedy worthy of shakespeare. it’s a masterpiece of its era, a film that feels as relevant today as it did upon its 2010 release.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.37151509523391724}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.0023977598175406456}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.6700509786605835}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.01125003769993782}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.9189092516899109}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -1.7982816696166992}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -3.2066785934148356e-05}, {"token": "